---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
---

<Base title="Run a receiver">
    <h1>Run a receiver</h1>
    <p>
        To run a receiver and join the demo, you only need docker installed. The
        receiver connects to the Flexicast source (
        <code>193.191.171.131:4435</code>) and receives heartbeats over
        multicast. If the receiver notices it isn't receiving multicast data
        natively, it will fallback on AMT.
    </p>

    <h2>Requirements</h2>
    <ul>
        <li>
            Docker, the container is built for arm64 and amd64 and has been
            tested on Linux and MacOS (27.2.1)
        </li>
        <li>
            Outbound UDP connectivity to the source (193.191.171.131:4435) and
            to the AMT relays (62.40.96.44:2268), and TCP 8080 to the source for
            the QLOG upload. NOTE: Some university networks may block QUIC
            connections, in which case you might have to try from a residential
            network. Do keep in mind that the IP addresses of the receivers are
            stored, but will not be shared with anyone and will only be used for
            data analysis.
        </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Start the container</h2>
    <pre><code>docker run --rm -it --init --network host --pull always \
  -e RUST_LOG=info -e SRC_PORT=4435 \
  corentindetry/fcquic-beacon-receiver:fcping 193.191.171.131 1</code></pre>
    <p>
        Or clone the{" "}
        <a href="https://github.com/IPNetworkingLab/multicast-ietf127-demo">
            repo
        </a>{" "}
        and run <code>./run_demo.sh</code>, which simply runs the same
        container. Extra arguments are passed to the receiver binary, e.g.
        <code>./run_demo.sh --pretty-print false</code>.
    </p>
    <p>
        To obtain more information, you can run the script with{" "}
        <code>RUST_LOG</code> set to <code>info</code> or <code>debug</code>.
    </p>
    <p>
        Use ctrl-c to stop the receiver. When the container is stopping, the
        QLOGs of the connection are sent over to our source server for analysis.
        You can disable qlog collection by setting
        <code>QLOGDIR</code> to an empty string (<code>-e QLOGDIR=</code>).
    </p>

    <h2>What you receive</h2>
    <p>
        Each heartbeat is a JSON message containing a session id, sequence
        number, timestamp, and interval,... Heartbeats are sent every 4 seconds,
        and every 5th heartbeat also carries a random 100 kilobytes payload, the
        sha256 hash of the payload is sent in the heartbeat to check integrity.
    </p>

    <h2>Check that it works</h2>
    <p>
        You should receive heartbeats within a few seconds. If the receiver
        fallsback to AMT, you should see the line{" "}
        <code>Left MULTICAST group 239.239.239.36</code>, or when running the
        receiver with <code>env RUST_LOG=info</code>, you should see a line{" "}
        <code>Left multicast group after creating AMT gateway</code>.
    </p>

    <h2>Data we collect</h2>
    <p>We will collect the following information about the connection:</p>
    <ul>
        <li>Non-anonymized IP address</li>
        <li>
            Complete session logs, QLOGs include all packets sent and received
            by the client at the transport level.
        </li>
        <li>AMT, Unicast fallback events</li>
        <li>Retransmissions,...</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
        This information will only be used for research purposes, and will allow
        us to improve our Flexicast QUIC implementation, find new potential
        issues, and gain insights only attainable from real deployments. TODO:
        retention period and contact.
    </p>
</Base>
